To evaluate the integral \( \int_{\frac{3\pi}{2}}^{\frac{7\pi}{2}} \frac{e^{\tan^{-1}(\cos(x))}}{e^{\tan^{-1}(\sin(x))} + e^{\tan^{-1}(\cos(x))}} \,dx \), we start by noting the interval from \( \frac{3\pi}{2} \) to \( \frac{7\pi}{2} \) is equivalent to an interval of length \( 2\pi \). This allows us to shift the variable and consider the integrand over a standard interval of \( 0 \) to \( 2\pi \).

We define the function \( f(x) = \frac{e^{\tan^{-1}(\cos x)}}{e^{\tan^{-1}(\sin x)} + e^{\tan^{-1}(\cos x)}} \). Substituting \( x = u + \frac{3\pi}{2} \) transforms the integral into the interval \( [0, 2\pi] \), leading to the integrand \( f_u(u) = \frac{e^{ \tan^{-1}(\sin u) }}{e^{ - \tan^{-1}(\cos u) } + e^{ \tan^{-1}(\sin u) }} \).

Through further analysis and symmetry considerations, we note that the integrand has periodicity properties. Specifically, we observe that for any \( x \), \( f(x) + f\left(x + \frac{\pi}{2}\right) = 1 \). This symmetry suggests that the integral over a full period \( 2\pi \) can be split into two integrals each of length \( \pi \), where each integral over \( \pi \) averages to \( \frac{\pi}{2} \).

Thus, integrating over the interval \( 0 \) to \( 2\pi \), the symmetry implies the integral must average to the value that satisfies \( f(x) + f\left(x + \frac{\pi}{2}\right) = 1 \), leading to the conclusion that the integral is \( \pi \).

The final answer is \(\boxed{\pi}\).<|im_end|>
