Given a profinite group \( G \), we consider the Grothendieck groups \( K(G, \mathbb{Z}_\ell) \) and \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \), defined as the Grothendieck groups of the derived categories of finitely generated \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-modules and \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \)-modules with continuous \( G \)-actions, respectively. The reduction map \([T] \mapsto [T \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}^L \mathbb{F}_\ell]\) is a group homomorphism.

The problem asks whether, for a \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-module \( T \) with a continuous \( G \)-action that is killed by some power of \( \ell \), the derived reduction \([T \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}^L \mathbb{F}_\ell]\) is zero in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \). 

Key considerations:
1. If \( T \) is killed by \( \ell^n \), it is a finite-dimensional \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \)-vector space, hence an \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \)-module.
2. The derived tensor product \( T \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell}^L \mathbb{F}_\ell \) simplifies to \( T \otimes_{\mathbb{F}_\ell} \mathbb{F}_\ell \), which is just \( T \) itself when considered in the derived category of \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \)-modules.
3. In \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \), the class \([T]\) is non-zero because \( T \) is a finite-dimensional \( \mathbb{F}_\ell \)-vector space, which contributes a non-trivial element in the derived category's Grothendieck group.

Counterexample:
- Take \( T = \mathbb{F}_\ell \), which is indeed a \( \mathbb{Z}_\ell \)-module killed by \( \ell \). The reduction map sends \( [\mathbb{F}_\ell] \) to \( [\mathbb{F}_\ell] \), which is non-zero in \( K(G, \mathbb{F}_\ell) \).

Thus, the reduction map does not send such \( T \) to zero, indicating the statement is false.

